<p>On 4 September 1807, after 113 days at sea, a twenty-five-year-old Scot named Robert Morrison came ashore at Macau. He had been sent by the London Missionary Society to learn Chinese, compile a dictionary, and translate the Bible. Within three days the Roman Catholic authorities in Macau had expelled him, and he moved upriver to the Thirteen Factories outside Canton, where he took a room in an American merchant's house and began the work.</p>

<p>He had already been told what the work would require. Before he landed, George Thomas Staunton laid out the situation for him without softening it. The local Chinese were forbidden by their own government to teach their language to anybody, on pain of death. No foreigner could remain in China except for purposes of trade. And the Portuguese-sponsored Catholic mission in Macau would oppose a Protestant arrival with everything it had.</p>

<p>The law was not theoretical. In 1759, a Chinese teacher named Liu Yabian had been publicly executed for teaching Chinese to James Flint, an East India Company employee, and for helping him write a petition. The Qing court followed the execution with the Precaution Regulations Against Foreign Barbarians, a set of rules designed to keep the language barrier in place. Teaching a foreigner to read Chinese was treated as a form of collusion with the enemy.</p>

