<h2>A Boy Who Left School at Fourteen</h2>

<p>Morrison was born in Bullers Green, near Morpeth in Northumberland, on 5 January 1782, the youngest of eight children. His father James was a Scottish farm laborer who moved the family to Newcastle when Robert was three and found steadier work in the shoe trade. His mother, Hannah Nicholson, was English, and both parents were active members of the Church of Scotland.</p>

<p>The detail that predicts everything else comes from his childhood. At the age of twelve he recited the entire 119th Psalm - 176 verses - from memory in front of his pastor without a single mistake. Whatever else the boy had, he had a capacity for holding large quantities of text in his head and refusing to let go.</p>

<p>At fourteen he left school and was apprenticed into his father's business, and for a couple of years he ran with a crowd his upbringing did not approve of. Around 1798 that changed. He described the turn himself:</p>

<blockquote>It was about five years ago that I was much awakened to a sense of sin ... Sin became a burden. It was then that I experienced a change of life, and, I trust, a change of heart, too. I broke off from my former careless company, and gave myself to reading, meditation and prayer.</blockquote>

<p>What followed is the part that matters for anyone reading this. He was doing manual labor twelve or fourteen hours a day in his father's shop. He still found one or two hours a day to read, and he read the handful of books he could get his hands on repeatedly, until they were worn. He kept the Bible or a commentary open on the counter while his hands worked.</p>

<p>In 1801 he began studying Latin, Greek, Hebrew, systematic theology and shorthand with a Presbyterian minister in Newcastle named W. Laidler. He was nineteen, working full time, and starting the classical languages from nothing. His parents opposed the direction he was taking. He did it anyway.</p>

<h2>London, and a Teacher From Canton</h2>

<p>In January 1803 he entered Hoxton Academy in London to train as a Congregationalist minister, and later went to David Bogue's academy at Gosport. He also studied medicine at St Bartholomew's Hospital under Dr Blair and astronomy at the Greenwich Observatory under Dr Hutton - the Society's reasoning being that these had served the early Jesuit missionaries in China and might serve him.</p>

<p>Then came the accident of a lifetime. The Society lodged him with a young Cantonese man studying English in London, whose name Morrison transcribed as Yong Sam-tak (probably Rong Sande). This was his first Chinese teacher, and the arrangement nearly ended in the first week: Morrison absent-mindedly burned a piece of paper with Chinese characters on it, which infuriated his teacher's sense of propriety, and the man left for three days. Morrison learned the lesson and from then on wrote his characters on a piece of tin that could be wiped clean.</p>

<p>Working from an early Chinese translation of the Gospels and a handwritten Latin-Chinese dictionary, he made what his biographers call considerable progress in speaking and writing one of the hardest languages in the world for an English speaker. He was still in England. He had not yet met the law.</p>

<h2>The Wall</h2>

<p>What he found in China was not difficulty. It was prohibition. His own journal records it with the flat exasperation of a man who could not believe what he was hearing:</p>

<blockquote>This shrewd and discerning people are absurd and unreasonable enough to consider it criminal for foreigners to know their language or possess their books, and still more to have the books of foreigners in their own possession ... My books relating to the language must be brought to Canton without the knowledge of the mandarins, Chinese books cannot be obtained but by stealth.</blockquote>

<p>That sentence describes an entire study method forced on him by law. He could not buy books openly. He could not be seen reading. He passed himself off as an American, because the Chinese authorities disliked and suspected Americans less than the English, and he kept his Chinese volumes out of sight in case his landlord or a visitor concluded that his business was mastering the language.</p>

<p>His first tutors were Catholics who had learned Latin from earlier missionaries. One, Abel Yun, taught him Mandarin. A scholar Morrison recorded as Ko Seen-sang worked with him on the written language and the classical texts. He also found a Mandarin teacher who demanded extortionate fees, and a man who bought him Chinese books and robbed him handsomely on the transaction. He lived in near-complete seclusion, afraid of being seen in the street, and his health collapsed to the point where he could barely walk across his narrow room.</p>

<p>He discovered another problem that will be familiar to anyone who has learned a standard variety and then met real speakers. The Mandarin he had been taught did not let him understand the common people of Canton, and translating scripture into the speech of a small aristocratic class was not what he had come for. So he learned Cantonese too - by talking to the people around him, badly at first, and by praying in broken Chinese, deliberately, so that he would be forced to build the sentences.</p>

<h2>Legitimacy, and a Roof That Fell In</h2>

<p>In February 1809 he married seventeen-year-old Mary Morton in Macau, and on the same day he was appointed translator to the East India Company at a salary of five hundred pounds a year. That appointment did more for his Chinese than any tutor had. It gave him a legal reason to be in Canton, a room inside the Thirteen Factories, and daily contact with the language at the highest and lowest registers - merchants, officials, ships' crews, servants, and the mandarins he had to write to.</p>

<p>It also gave him a cover story. A grammar and a dictionary were not Christian publications. When the Jiaqing Emperor issued an edict in 1812 adding Christianity to the list of banned superstitions, making the printing of Chinese Christian books a capital crime, Morrison forwarded a translation of the proclamation to London and told the Directors he intended to go quietly and resolutely forward. The dictionary was, in a quite literal sense, his license to keep working.</p>

<h2>Sixteen Years, 4,595 Pages</h2>

<p>He finished a Chinese grammar in 1812 and sent it to Bengal to be printed, then heard nothing for three anxious years. It came back well printed, in 1815, at 280 pages. Meanwhile the dictionary was growing.</p>

<p><em>A Dictionary of the Chinese Language, in Three Parts</em> appeared between 1815 and 1823. It ran to <strong>4,595 pages in six quarto volumes and carried 47,035 head characters</strong>, drawn from the 1716 Kangxi Dictionary. Part I was Chinese-English, arranged by the 214 Kangxi radicals. Part II was Chinese-English, arranged alphabetically. Part III was English-Chinese, also alphabetical - the first bidirectional Chinese-English reference work ever made.</p>

<p>He was not working from comfort. His first child, James, was born in March 1811 and died the same day; Morrison had to arrange the burial on a mountainside himself while his wife was dangerously ill, because the local community objected to the interment. His assistants robbed him. Letters from England came seldom. He worked on anyway, for what his contemporaries described as extraordinary perseverance, industry and ingenuity.</p>

<p>In 1819, comparing his progress to the thirty years another scholar had predicted the task would take, he wrote that the estimate "will be reduced to one-third of that period". He was off by a little, and he knew it, and he kept going.</p>

<h3>The Flaws That Came From His Tutors</h3>

<p>Two of the dictionary's errors are worth naming, because both came from the conditions of his learning rather than from carelessness. His tonal markings followed Middle Chinese - the system in the Kangxi Dictionary and the rhyming dictionaries he used - rather than the tones actually spoken in his own century. And because of the tutors available to him, his transcriptions were based on Nanjing Mandarin rather than the Beijing dialect. He also failed to distinguish aspirated consonants, so the same pronunciation was given for <em>táo</em> (peach) and <em>dào</em> (way).</p>

<p>These are the fingerprints of a self-directed learner working from written sources in a country that would not let him speak to teachers freely. The great dictionary was built partly out of books and partly out of whatever access he could get, and the seams show.</p>

<h2>The Day the Language Saved a Life</h2>

<p>One episode from his years in Canton has nothing to do with scholarship and everything to do with why the language mattered. A criminal named Tsae-kung-chaou had been sentenced to beheading, and nobody at the tribunal could understand the dialect he spoke. Morrison came forward and interpreted the man's defense in Fokien dialect for the Chinese judges. The sentence was not carried out.</p>

<p>He had learned that dialect in the same unglamorous way he learned everything else - by listening to people who spoke it and asking them what they had said.</p>

<p>In 1817 he went with Lord Amherst's embassy to Beijing as the Company's interpreter, which took him through cities and country districts he would never otherwise have seen and enlarged his knowledge of the country considerably. In 1818 he helped found the Anglo-Chinese College at Malacca, an institution designed to teach Chinese to Europeans and English to Chinese, which later moved to Hong Kong and still exists as Ying Wa College. He completed the whole Chinese Bible with William Milne in 1823, twelve years after the New Testament was finished.</p>

<p>By 1832 he could write, to a friend, something that reads like a man closing a ledger:</p>

<blockquote>There is now in Canton a state of society, in respect of Chinese, totally different from what I found in 1807. Chinese scholars, missionary students, English presses and Chinese Scriptures, with public worship of God, have all grown up since that period. I have served my generation, and must the Lord know when I fall asleep.</blockquote>

<p>He died on 1 August 1834, in China, and is buried in Macau.</p>

<h2>Four Habits Worth Stealing</h2>

<p>Morrison is not usually filed under self-education, because he had an academy and a society behind him. But the language itself he learned the way a self-taught person learns: in secret, from whatever source would take him, without a curriculum, and against an authority that had made his textbooks contraband.</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>He worked from a text he was willing to read a hundred times.</strong> The boy who memorized 176 verses read and re-read the few books he owned until they fell apart. Scarcity forced depth, and depth is what the dictionary later ran on.</li>
<li><strong>He started before he was ready.</strong> He was translating scripture and writing a grammar while his Chinese was still bad enough that he prayed in broken sentences on purpose. He did not wait for fluency to begin the work; the work is how he got fluent.</li>
<li><strong>He built the reference he could not find.</strong> Sixteen years of lexicography is an extreme version of a habit any learner can copy at a smaller scale: when the resource you need does not exist, make a bad version of it yourself, and the making teaches you more than using it would.</li>
<li><strong>He let a native speaker correct his instincts.</strong> Yong Sam-tak's fury over a burned scrap of paper changed how Morrison handled Chinese characters for the rest of his life. He kept the correction.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What the Secret Cost</h2>

<p>The popular version of Morrison's story is a hero narrative about a missionary who overcame obstacles. The more useful version is about what prohibition does to learning. He had to learn a language from people who could be killed for teaching him, using books he could only obtain by stealth, in a country that would not let him stay except as a merchant's clerk. The result was the most important Chinese-English dictionary of the century, and it was measurably wrong in ways that a single term of open study in Beijing would have corrected.</p>

<p>Nobody today learns under that constraint. Which means the excuse most of us use - no teacher, no resources, no immersion - is being measured against a man who had none of those and produced 4,595 pages anyway. He was asked, shortly after arriving, whether he expected to have any spiritual impact on the Chinese. His answer was blunt: "No sir, but I expect God will."</p>

<p>He had already prayed for a posting where the difficulties were greatest and, to all human appearances, the most insurmountable. He got one. Then he bought a piece of tin, and started writing characters on it.</p>

